# Hostile audit — Target C Section 131 component-ledger amplification

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_section131_component_ledger_amplification.md`
**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only Type-II endpoint scope.** C-II remains open.

## 1. Re-derive the finite inequality independently

Canonical Section 75 gives

\[
\sum_r\delta_r+R_I+s_B+3k=6+e_{BB}+2s_C,
\qquad R_I,s_B\ge0.
\]

Canonical harmonic localization gives `d_r<=delta_r+2`. Hence

\[
\sum_r d_r
\le 6+e_{BB}+2s_C-3k+2c_I.
\tag{A131.1}
\]

Proof-only Section 125 gives, in Type II,

\[
e_{BB}=q-m,
\qquad
c_I+s_C=m+k-2.
\]

Substitution into (A131.1) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_r d_r
&\le6+q-m+2(m+k-2-c_I)-3k+2c_I\\
&=q+m-k+2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{q+m-k\ge\sum_r d_r-2.}
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.  The `c_I` terms cancel exactly.  No asymptotic component-count estimate is used in the finite theorem.

## 2. Endpoint insertion

The repaired and hostile-audited Section 130 gives, only on its stated Section-118 endpoint/common-cap branch,

\[
\sum_r d_r\ge(S_*-o(1))D,
\qquad
S_*=2.798596312209462\ldots .
\]

Thus

\[
q+m-k\ge S_*D-o(D).
\tag{A131.2}
\]

**Verdict:** PASS at the same proof-only endpoint scope.  The audit uses the binding Section-130 limit repair, not the rejected exact-maximal-arc shortcut.

## 3. Independent check of the second inequality

The corrected Sections-124/127 payment used by the Section-130 hostile audit is

\[
3(h-s_Z)-2s_C
\ge(S_*+c_*)D-o(D),
\qquad
c_*={\pi\over4}(2-\sqrt3).
\tag{A131.3}
\]

Since `h-s_Z=q-k` exactly and

\[
s_C=m+k-2-c_I,
\]

(A131.3) is

\[
3q-2m-5k+4+2c_I
\ge(S_*+c_*)D-o(D).
\tag{A131.4}
\]

Canonical Section 75 gives `c_I=O(D^(2/3))=o(D)`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
3q-2m-5k
\ge(S_*+c_*)D-o(D).
}
\tag{A131.5}
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.  Here, unlike the finite inequality of Section 131.1, sublinearity of `c_I` is genuinely used.  Its sign is handled correctly: the `+2c_I` term is absorbed into the `o(D)` error, not simply dropped.

## 4. Elimination and arithmetic

Twice (A131.2) plus (A131.5) gives

\[
5q-7k
\ge(3S_*+c_*)D-o(D).
\]

Using

\[
q=h+d_H,
\qquad
k=d_H+s_Z,
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
5h-2d_H-7s_Z
\ge(3S_*+c_*)D-o(D).
}
\]

The constant is

\[
3S_*+c_*
=8.60623574024762\ldots,
\]

so nonnegativity of `d_H,s_Z` gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf h/D\ge1.72124714804952\ldots .
}
\]

The coefficient and inequality directions are correct.

## 5. Regression against recently withdrawn interface claims

The proof does **not** use either recently rejected statement

\[
(q-k)/D\ge1.9046256\ldots
\]

or

\[
(2h-q)/D\ge0.6676585\ldots .
\]

Its only canonical global component input is Section 75, and its proof-only inputs are Sections 125, 124/127, and the repaired/audited Section 130.  Therefore the main-branch correction withdrawing those interface claims does not invalidate Section 131.

## 6. Scope and remaining obstruction

The result is not an all-Type-II theorem.  The numerical endpoint uses the Section-130 common-cap endpoint branch.  It does not address Type I and does not itself contradict the diameter-thrackle upper range.

What it changes is the endpoint geometry: any surviving endpoint countersequence must have diameter-edge density at least `1.721247...`, far above the universal `2rho=1.47817...` floor.  A closing composition must now exploit this dense thrackle together with the near-circular loaded side sequence or the gap-spanning harmonic cells.

**Final verdict:** PASS as a proof-only endpoint theorem.  Theorem state unchanged; C-II remains open.
